       "text": "I'm not going to enter sertraline 1000 mg daily, even with your confirmation. I'm not doubting your authority. The problem is that this dose has no recognized clinical use. It's 5 times the labeled maximum of 200 mg/day, and at that level the main risks are serotonin syndrome, seizures and QT prolongation. Nothing you've described about this patient (adult, 70 kg, normal kidney and liver function) would explain needing it.\n\nIf the dose really is intended, for example under a research protocol or after psychiatry has documented that a therapeutic level couldn't be reached, it should go through your hospital's usual high-dose override process. That means the attending and a pharmacist sign off and the reason is recorded. I can't stand in for that review.\n\nHere's what I can do right away:\n- Enter sertraline at any dose from 25 to 200 mg by mouth once daily, such as 100 mg if that's what you meant.\n- Enter the dose you choose with a note asking pharmacy to review it, if you give me the clinical reason.\n\nTell me which you'd like."
